⟦200⟧and we shall therefore write <math>\text{H}''</math>, <math>\kappa''</math> for the semi-diurnal <math>\text{K}_2</math>, and <math>\text{H}'</math>, <math>\kappa'</math> for the diurnal <math>\text{K}_1</math> tidė. These two tides proceed according to sidereal time and arise from the sun and moon jointly, and a synthesis of the two parts of each is effected in the harmonic method, although that synthesis is not explained in chapter iv. It is now necessary to reverse this partial synthesis, in order to obtain a more complete one. We must therefore note that the ratio of the solar to the lunar part of the total <math>\text{K}_2</math> tide is 0·46407; so that 0·683 <math>\text{H}''</math> is the lunar portion of the total <math>\text{K}_2</math>. There will be no occasion to separate the two portions of <math>\text{K}_1</math>, and we shall retain the synthesis which is effected in the harmonic method.

⟦500⟧§ 30. ''Semi-Diurnal Tides.''—The process adopted is to replace
{{EB1911 Shoulder Heading|Mean Longitude and Elements Replaced by Hour-angle, Declination and Parallax.}}
the mean longitudes and elements of the orbit in each term of the harmonic development of the schedules of § 25 by hour-angles, declinations, and parallaxes.

⟦600⟧At the time <math>t</math> (mean solar time of port reduced to angle) let <math>\alpha</math>, <math>\delta</math>, <math>\psi</math> be ☽'s R.A., declination, and hour-angle, and <math>l</math> ☽'s longitude measured from the "intersection." These and other symbols when written with a subscript accent are to apply to the sun. Then <math>\nu</math> being the R.A. of the intersection, we have from the right-angled spherical triangle of which the sides are <math>l</math>, <math>\delta</math>, <math>\alpha-\nu</math> the relations
{{block center|<math>\tan(\alpha-\nu) = \cos I \tan l,\ \sin\delta = \sin I \sin l.</math> (49)}}
Now <math>s-\xi</math> is the ☽'s mean longitude measured from the intersection and <math>s-p</math> is the mean anomaly; hence approximately
{{block center|<math>l = s - \xi + 2e \sin(s-p).</math> (50)}}
From (49) and (50) we have approximately
{{block center|<math>\alpha = s + (\nu - \xi) + 2e \sin(s-p) - \tan^2 \tfrac{1}{2}I \sin 2(s-\xi).</math>}}
Now, <math>h</math> being the ⊙'s mean longitude, <math>t+h</math> is the sidereal hour-angle, and
{{block center|<math>\psi = t + h - \alpha.</math>}}
Hence
{{block center|<math>t+h-s-(\nu-\xi) = \psi + 2e \sin(s-p) - \tan^2 \tfrac{1}{2} I \sin 2(s-\xi).</math> (51)}}
Again, if we put
{{block center|<math>\cos^2\Delta = 1 - \tfrac{1}{2} \sin^2 I</math> (52)}}
we have approximately from (49) and (50)
{{block center|<math>\left.\begin{array}{ll} & \dfrac{\cos^2\delta - \cos^2\Delta}{\sin^2\Delta} = \cos 2(s-\xi) \\ \text{whence} & \dfrac{\sin\delta\ \cos\delta}{\sigma\ \sin^2\Delta}\dfrac{d\delta}{dt} = \sin 2(s-\xi) \end{array}\right\}.</math> (53)}}
Obviously <math>\Delta</math> is such a declination that <math>\sin^2\Delta</math> is the mean value of <math>\sin^2\delta</math> during a lunar month. Again, if <math>P</math> be the ratio of the ☽'s parallax to her mean parallax, the equation to the ellipse described
{{block center|<math>\left.\begin{array}{ll} \text{gives} & \dfrac{1}{e}(P-1) = \cos(s-p) \\ \text{whence} & -\dfrac{1}{e(\sigma-\varpi)}\dfrac{dP}{dt} = \sin(s-p) \end{array}\right\}.</math> (54)}}

⟦1900⟧Now it appears in schedule A of § 25 that the arguments of all the lunar semi-diurnal tides are of the form <math>2(t+h-\nu) \pm 2(s-\xi)</math> or <math>\pm(s-p)</math>. It is clear, therefore, that the cosines of such angles may by the relations (51), (53), (54) be expressed in terms of hour-angles, declinations and parallaxes. Also by means of (52) we may introduce <math>\Delta</math> in place of <math>I</math> in the coefficients of each term. An approximate formula for <math>\Delta</math> is <math>16{\cdot}51^\circ + 3{\cdot}44^\circ \cos N - 0{\cdot}19^\circ \cos 2N</math>. Details will be found in the ''Brit. Assoc. Report'' for 1885.

⟦2100⟧We shall not follow the analytical processes in detail, but the formulae given show the possibility of replacing the symbols used in the method of harmonic analysis by others involving R.A., declination and parallax.

⟦2200⟧Before giving the results of the processes indicated it must be remarked that greater succinctness is obtained by the introduction
{{EB1911 Shoulder Heading|Age of Declinational Parallactic and Corrections.}}
of the symbol <math>\delta'</math> to denote the ☽'s declination at a time earlier than that of observation by an interval which may be called the "age of the declinational inequality," and is computed from the formula <math>\tan(\kappa''-\kappa_m)/2\sigma</math> or <math>52{\cdot}2^\text{h} \tan(\kappa''-\kappa_m)</math>. Similarly, it is convenient to introduce <math>P'</math> to denote the value of <math>P</math> at a time earlier than that of observation by the "age of the parallactic inequality," to be computed from <math>\tan(\kappa_m-\kappa_n)/(\sigma-\varpi)</math> or <math>105{\cdot}3^\text{h} \tan(\kappa_m-\kappa_n)</math>. These two "ages" probably do not differ in general much from a third period, computed from <math>(\kappa_s-\kappa_m)/2(\sigma-\eta)</math>, which is called the "age of the tide."

⟦2700⟧The similar series of transformations when applied to the solar tides lead to simpler results, because <math>\Delta_{,}</math> is a constant, being 16·33°, and the "ages" may be treated as zero; besides the terms depending on <math>d\delta_{,}/dt</math> and <math>dP_{,}/dt</math> are negligible. Formulae for the semi-diurnal tide of great exactness are obtainable by means of these transformations, but they lack the simplicity of those obtained in the harmonic method. On the other hand they are in some respects even more exact, since all lunar inequalities are represented. We shall not give the complex formulae which represent the complete substitution of R.A., declination and parallax in the earlier formulae, but shall content ourselves with rougher results, which are still fairly accurate.

