⟦200⟧Let us write them
{{block center|<math>\text{M} = \text{P}'^3\frac{\cos^2\nabla}{\cos^2\Delta_,}\text{H}_m + \frac{\cos^2\delta' - \cos^2\Delta}{\sin^2\Delta_,}0{\cdot}683\,\text{H}''\cos(\kappa''-\kappa_m),</math>}}
{{block center|<math>2\mu = \kappa_m + \frac{\cos^2\delta' - \cos^2\Delta}{\sin^2\Delta_,}0{\cdot}683\frac{\text{H}''}{\text{H}_m}\sin(\kappa''-\kappa_m),</math>}}
{{block center|<math>\text{M}_, = \text{P}_,{}^3\frac{\cos^2\delta_,}{\cos^2\Delta_,}\text{H}_s,</math>}}
{{block center|<math>2\mu_, = \kappa_s.</math> (55)}}
Then we find that the height <math>\text{h}_2</math> of the complete lunar and solar semi-diurnal tide is represented with a fair degree of approximation by
{{block center|<math>\text{h}_2 = \text{M}\cos 2(\psi-\mu) + \text{M}_,\cos 2(\psi_,-\mu_,).</math> (56)}}

⟦600⟧The first of these is the lunar tide, and it will be observed that the height <math>\text{M}</math> depends on the cube of the moon's parallax at a time
{{EB1911 Shoulder Heading|Synthesis giving one Lunar and one Solar Tide.}}
earlier than that of observation by "the age of the parallactic inequality," and that it depends also on the moon's declination at a time earlier by "the age of the declinational inequality." The phase of the tide, represented by the angle <math>2\mu</math>, also has a declinational inequality.

⟦800⟧The second term is the solar tide, and it also has parallactic and declinational inequalities.

⟦900⟧The formulae (55), (56) have been used in an example of the computation of a tide-table given in the ''Admiralty Scientific Manual'' (1886).

⟦1000⟧§ 31. ''Synthesis of Lunar and Solar Semi-Diurnal Tides.''—Let <math>\text{A}</math> be the excess of ☽'s over ☉'s R.A., so that
{{block center|<math>\text{A} = \alpha-\alpha_,,</math>}}
{{block center|<math>\psi_, = \psi + \text{A},</math>}}
{{block center|and <math>\text{h}_2 = \text{M}\cos 2(\psi-\mu)+\text{M}_,\cos 2(\psi+\text{A}-\mu_,).</math> (57)}}
The synthesis is then completed by writing
{{block center|<math>\text{H}\cos 2(\mu-\phi) = \text{M}+\text{M}_,\cos 2(\text{A}-\mu_,+\mu),</math>}}
{{block center|<math>\text{H}\sin 2(\mu-\phi) = \text{M}_,\sin 2(\text{A}-\mu_,+\mu),</math>}}
{{block center|so that <math>\text{h}_2 = \text{H}\cos 2(\psi-\phi).</math> (58)}}
Then <math>\text{H}</math> is the height of the total semi-diurnal tide and <math>\phi/(\gamma-d\alpha/dt)</math> or approximately <math>\phi/(\gamma-\sigma)</math> or <math>\tfrac{2}{29}\phi</math>, when <math>\phi</math> is given in
{{EB1911 Shoulder Heading|Synthesis to obtain Single Term.}}
degrees, is the "interval" from the moon's transit to high water.

⟦1400⟧The formulae for <math>\text{H}</math> and <math>\phi</math> may be written
{{block center|<math>\text{H} = \sqrt{\{\text{M}^2+\text{M}_,^2+2\text{MM}_,\cos 2(\text{A}-\mu_,+\mu)\}}</math>}}
{{block center|<math>\tan 2(\mu-\phi) = \frac{\text{M}_,\sin 2(\text{A}-\mu_,+\mu)}{\text{M}+\text{M}_,\cos 2(\text{A}-\mu_,+\mu)}.</math> (59)}}
Since <math>\text{A}</math> goes through its period in a lunation, it follows that <math>\text{H}</math> and <math>\phi</math> have inequalities with a period of half a lunation.
{{EB1911 Shoulder Heading|Fortnightly Inequality.}}
These are called the "fortnightly inequalities in the height and interval.

⟦1700⟧Spring tide obviously occurs when <math>\text{A} = \mu_,-\mu</math>. Since the mean value of <math>\text{A}</math> is <math>s-h</math> (the difference of the mean longitudes), and since the mean values of <math>\mu</math> and <math>\mu_,</math> are <math>\tfrac{1}{2}\kappa_m</math>, <math>\tfrac{1}{2}\kappa_s</math>, it follows that the mean value of the period elapsing after full moon and change of moon up to spring tide is <math>(\kappa_s-\kappa_m)/2(\sigma-\eta)</math>. The association of spring tide with full and change is obvious, and a fiction has been adopted by which it is held that spring tide is generated in those configurations of the moon and sun, but takes some time to reach the port of observation. Accordingly <math>(\kappa_s-\kappa_m)/2(\sigma-\eta)</math> has
{{EB1911 Shoulder Heading|Age of Tide.}}
been called the "age of the tide." The average age is about 36 hours as far as observations have yet been made. The age of the tide appears not in general to differ very much from the ages of the declinational and parallactic inequalities.

⟦2200⟧In computing a tide-table it is found practically convenient not to use <math>\text{A}</math>, which is the difference of R.A.'s at the unknown time of high-water, but to refer the tide to <math>\text{A}_0</math>, the difference of R.A.'s at the time of the moon's transit. It is clear that <math>\text{A}_0</math> is the apparent time of the moon's transit reduced to angle at 15° per hour. We have already remarked that <math>\phi/(\gamma-d\alpha/dt)</math> is the interval from transit to high-water, and hence at high-water
{{block center|<math>\text{A} = \text{A}_0 + \frac{d\alpha/dt - d\alpha_,/dt}{\gamma - d\alpha_,/dt}\phi.</math> (60)}}

⟦2500⟧As an approximation we may attribute to all the quantities in the second term their mean values, and we then
{{EB1911 Shoulder Heading|Reference to Moon's Transit.}}
have
{{block center|<math>\text{A} = \text{A}_0 + \frac{\sigma-\eta}{\gamma-\sigma}\mu</math>}}
{{block center|and <math>\text{A} - \mu_, + \mu = \text{A}_0 - \mu_, + \frac{\gamma-\eta}{\gamma-\sigma}\mu = \text{A}_0 - \mu_, + \tfrac{30}{29}\mu.</math> (61)}}

⟦2700⟧This approximate formula (61) may be used in computing from (59) the fortnightly inequality in the "height" and "interval."

⟦2800⟧In this investigation we have supposed that the declinational and parallactic corrections are applied to the lunar and solar tides before their synthesis; but it is obvious that the process may be reversed, and that we may form a table of the fortnightly inequality based on mean values <math>\text{H}_m</math> and <math>\text{H}_s</math> and afterwards apply corrections. This is the process usually adopted, but it is less exact. The labour of computing the fortnightly inequality, especially by graphical methods, is not great, and the plan here suggested seems preferable.

